    "summary": "Master the 4 fundamental text splitting strategies, understand why chunk overlap preserves cross-boundary semantic meaning, and explore local embedding models like BGE and Sentence Transformers.",
    "content": "### 1. What is Chunking and Why Does Chunk Size Matter? (The Definitive Definition)\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Formal Technical Definition (Exam Standard):**  \n> **Chunking (Text Splitting)** is the algorithmic process of dividing long continuous source documents into smaller, semantically self-contained textual units optimized for dense vector embedding, index storage, and prompt context window injection.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **\ud83d\udca1 In Simple Words:**  \n> If you give an AI a 500-page textbook, it gets overwhelmed and forgets facts in the middle. Chunking cuts that textbook into neat index cards (about 300 words each). That way, when you ask a question, the search engine only pulls out the 3 cards you actually need!\n\n```text\nTHE 3 TEXT SPLITTING STRATEGIES COMPARED\n1. Fixed-Size Chunking:\n   [ \"The capital city of France is Paris, wh\" ] [ \"ich is known for its world-famous ar\" ]\n   \u25b2\u2500\u2500 Word bisected right down the middle! Semantic meaning destroyed.\n\n2. Recursive Character Splitting (Production Standard):\n   [ Paragraph 1: Complete logical thought ] \u2500\u2500\u25ba [ Paragraph 2: Complete logical thought ]\n   \u25b2\u2500\u2500 Splits on \\n\\n, then \\n, then spaces. Never cuts words in half!\n\n3. Semantic Chunking (Dynamic Embedding Threshold):\n   [ Sentence A ] [ Sentence B ] \u2500\u2500(Cosine Similarity = 0.92)\u2500\u2500\u25ba KEEP IN SAME CHUNK\n   [ Sentence B ] [ Sentence C ] \u2500\u2500(Cosine Similarity = 0.31)\u2500\u2500\u25ba SPLIT INTO NEW CHUNK!\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. The 4 Text Splitting Strategies\n\n#### 1. Fixed-Size Chunking\nDivides text strictly by a rigid token or character counter (e.g., cut every 500 characters).\n* **Pros:** Computationally trivial and blisteringly fast.\n* **Cons (Exam Hazard):** Arbitrarily bisects words, sentences, and code blocks in half, corrupting semantic meaning.\n\n#### 2. Recursive Character Splitting (Industry Standard)\nSplits text using a priority list of hierarchical separators: `[\"\\n\\n\", \"\\n\", \" \", \"\"]`.\n* **How it works:** Tries to split on double newlines (paragraphs) first. If a paragraph is still larger than `chunk_size`, it splits on single newlines (sentences). If still too large, it splits on spaces (words).\n* **Benefit:** Keeps paragraphs and sentences intact wherever possible.\n\n#### 3. Semantic Chunking (Meaning-Based Boundaries)\nInstead of counting characters, it measures the semantic shift between adjacent sentences:\n1. Breaks document into individual sentences.\n2. Computes embedding vectors for adjacent sentence pairs.\n3. Calculates cosine distance between sentence $i$ and sentence $i+1$.\n4. Places a chunk boundary whenever distance exceeds a dynamic threshold (e.g., 95th percentile of distances).\n\n#### 4. Document-Structure Chunking (Markdown / Code / HTML)\nSplits text by syntactic structural tokens (e.g., `# Header 1`, `## Header 2`, ````python`, `<table>`).\n* **Benefit:** Keeps code blocks, markdown tables, and section headers strictly together.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Why Chunk Overlap is Required\n\n```text\nTHE CHUNK OVERLAP SAFETY NET\nWithout Overlap:\n[ Chunk 1: Patient was prescribed 50mg of Atenolol daily. ] [ Chunk 2: However, if systolic BP drops below 90, discontinue immediately. ]\n\u25b2\u2500\u2500 Seam cuts condition from prescription! Answering query using Chunk 1 alone causes medical disaster!\n\nWith 20% Overlap:\n[ Chunk 1: Patient was prescribed 50mg of Atenolol daily. However, if systolic BP drops... ]\n[ Chunk 2: ...prescribed 50mg of Atenolol daily. However, if systolic BP drops below 90, discontinue immediately. ]\n\u25b2\u2500\u2500 Overlap guarantees both chunks preserve the safety condition!\n```\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> **High-Yield Exam Concept:** What is the exact purpose of chunk overlap?  \n> Chunk overlap (typically 10% to 20% of `chunk_size`) guarantees that semantic information spanning across chunk boundaries is not severed. It ensures that qualifying clauses, pronouns, and conditions are retained in both adjacent chunks.\n\n---\n\n### High-Yield Exam Takeaways\n\n* **Recursive Splitting Rule:** `RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter` is the production standard because it preserves document hierarchy (paragraphs $\to$ sentences $\to$ words) rather than slicing text blindly.\n* **Overlap Sizing:** Overlap is typically set to 10\u201320% of chunk size (e.g., 50-token overlap for a 500-token chunk). Overlaps $>30\\%$ waste vector database storage and inject redundant tokens into prompt contexts.\n* **Language Specificity:** Markdown text splitters keep header breadcrumbs (e.g., `# Architecture > ## Databases`) attached to every child chunk, providing vital context for retrieval."
