    "summary": "Master why pure vector and pure keyword search fail in production, understand BM25 TF-IDF mechanics, calculate Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF) with k=60, and build hybrid search pipelines.",
    "content": "### 1. What is Hybrid Retrieval? (The Definitive Definition)\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Formal Technical Definition (Exam Standard):**  \n> **Hybrid Retrieval** is a search architecture that concurrently executes **sparse lexical keyword search** (e.g., BM25) and **dense semantic vector search** (e.g., bi-encoder embeddings) over the same document corpus, combining their disparate candidate rankings using a rank-based fusion algorithm like **Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF)**.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> **\ud83d\udca1 In Simple Words:**  \n> Dense vector search understands *concepts* (it knows \"automobile\" means \"car\"), but it often stumbles on exact part numbers or rare acronyms (like \"Error Code 0x80070005\"). Keyword search (BM25) is the opposite: it's an expert at finding exact codes, but doesn't understand synonyms. Hybrid search runs both searches at the same time and combines their results so you get the best of both worlds!\n\n```text\nHYBRID SEARCH & RECIPROCAL RANK FUSION (RRF)\n                 \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n                 \u2502                      User Query                        \u2502\n                 \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n                                     \u2502                \u2502\n             \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518                \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n             \u25bc                                                                \u25bc\n\u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510                                      \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n\u2502   Sparse Search (BM25)  \u2502                                      \u2502   Dense Vector Search   \u2502\n\u2502 Exact keywords & codes  \u2502                                      \u2502 Conceptual & semantic   \u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518                                      \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n             \u2502                                                                \u2502\n             \u25bc                                                                \u25bc\n   [ BM25 Ranked List ]                                             [ Vector Ranked List ]\n   1. Doc B (Score: 18.2)                                           1. Doc A (Score: 0.94)\n   2. Doc A (Score: 12.1)                                           2. Doc C (Score: 0.88)\n   3. Doc D (Score: 8.4)                                            3. Doc B (Score: 0.81)\n             \u2502                                                                \u2502\n             \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510                \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n                                     \u25bc                \u25bc\n                         \u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n                         \u2502   Reciprocal Rank Fusion (k = 60)      \u2502\n                         \u2502 RRF(d) = \u03a3 1 / (60 + rank(d))          \u2502\n                         \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n                                             \u25bc\n                                 [ Merged Unified Ranking ]\n                                 1. Doc A (High in both!)\n                                 2. Doc B\n                                 3. Doc C\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF) Formula & Mechanics\n\nWhen merging BM25 and vector search, you cannot simply add their raw scores together because BM25 produces unbounded scores ($[0, \\infty)$) while cosine similarity produces bounded scores ($[0, 1]$). Adding them directly leads to score scale bias.\n\n**Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF)** solves this by looking strictly at **positions (ranks)** rather than raw scores:\n\n$$RRF(d \\in D) = \\sum_{m \\in M} \frac{1}{k + r_m(d)}$$\n\n* $M$: The set of retrieval systems (e.g., BM25 and Dense Vector).\n* $r_m(d)$: The 1-based rank position of document $d$ in retrieval system $m$.\n* $k$: A smoothing constant (standard industry default: **$k = 60$**).\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> **High-Yield Exam Concept:** Why is $k = 60$ used in RRF?  \n> The smoothing constant $k = 60$ prevents high-ranking items from dominating the score completely. It ensures that a document appearing at rank #1 in one system doesn't automatically overpower a document that appears at rank #2 across both systems.\n\n---\n\n### High-Yield Exam Takeaways\n\n* **The Failure Mode of Pure Vectors:** Dense embeddings fail on exact alphanumeric codes, SKUs, error hashes, and rare domain acronyms.\n* **The Failure Mode of Pure BM25:** Lexical search fails on vocabulary mismatch (e.g., query asks for \"cardiac arrest\" but document says \"heart attack\").\n* **RRF Invariance:** RRF is immune to score distribution differences because it only uses ordinal ranks."
